$ | $1.350$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86f614ee-dea5-5083-b18b-711f3e2bd84f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161667 #edb/ref 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X \\ge x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.99 \\\\  & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim T_{11},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=11.$\n\n| $k\\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.010}$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}2.718}$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n| $13$ | $1.350$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\ge {\\color{red}2.718}) = {\\color{blue}0.010}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.718.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161669 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161669 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X \\sim T_{13},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X| \\leq x) = 0.9.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n| $13$ | $1.350$ | $1.771$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f694b6c-8ece-5e51-9618-5158e23521fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161669 #edb/ref 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|X|>x) & =1-P(|X|\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.9 \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X| > x) = 0.1$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X > x) = 0.1 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nP(X > x) = 0.05.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim T_{13},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=13.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n| $13$ | $1.350$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}1.771}$ | $2.160$ | $2.650$ | $3.012$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq {\\color{red}1.771}) = {\\color{blue}0.050}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.771.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1039 "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a T-Score" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162100 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2b7e6407-bb34-5603-b325-580ac32f8a9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162101 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162101 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162101 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162101 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500300 #edb/ref